295	# Sidecar protocol schema
296	
297	`sidecar-v1.schema.json` is the JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) of the Nexus sidecar protocol, version 1: the newline-delimited JSON a client exchanges with the Node sidecar. `$defs.SidecarEvent` is every message the sidecar sends, `$defs.SidecarCommand` every message a client sends. Each is a `oneOf` over the message variants, told apart by the constant `type`; every message also carries `v`, the protocol version.
298	
299	The file is generated from the types in `cli/src/protocol.ts` by the TypeScript type checker. Do not edit it by hand: change the types, then regenerate.
300	
301	```bash
302	cd cli
303	node scripts/gen_protocol_schema.mjs           # rewrite docs/protocol/sidecar-v1.schema.json
304	node scripts/gen_protocol_schema.mjs --check   # exit 1 when the schema or the ledger is stale
305	```
306	
307	`cli/test/protocol_schema.test.ts` runs the same comparison, so CI fails when a protocol type changes and the schema does not.
308	
309	## Recording a version decision
310	
311	`VERSIONS.json` is the version ledger: entries `{ "protocol", "schemaSha256", "note" }`, newest last. The newest entry must carry the SHA-256 of the checked-in schema and the current `PROTOCOL_VERSION`. A type change therefore needs two things: a regenerated schema and a new ledger entry whose `note` states the decision, either the same version because the change is additive and compatible (a new optional field, a new message an older peer ignores), or a bumped `PROTOCOL_VERSION`. Append entries; leave earlier ones as they are.
312	
313	## Extra properties are allowed
314	
315	No object in the schema sets `additionalProperties: false`. A newer sidecar may add an optional field to an event, and an older client must ignore it, so a schema that forbade unknown fields would reject messages the protocol means to accept. Commands are open for the same reason in the other direction: `parseCommand` reads the fields it knows and drops the rest. The schema checks what is required and the type of every field it names. `additionalProperties` appears only where a type has an index signature, where it gives the type of every value.
316	
317	A tuple (`ReceiptRow`) is the exception: its length is fixed, as in the type.
318	
319	## What the generator does with each type
320	
321	- An interface or type alias declared under `cli/src` is written once under `$defs` and referenced with `$ref`. An instantiated generic (`Pick<SlashSpec, ...>`) and an intersection that forms a message (`models.catalog`) are written out in place.
322	- A union of string literals is an `enum`; `T | null` is `anyOf` with `{"type": "null"}`; an optional property is left out of `required`.
323	- JSDoc comments on types and properties become `description`.
324	- Definitions, properties, `required` lists, enum values and variants are sorted, so the output is the same on every run.
325	- A type with no JSON form (a function, an enum, a tuple with optional elements) stops the generator with an error naming the property.
326	
327	## The `nexus run --json` document
328	
329	`run-result-v1.schema.json` is the JSON Schema of the one document `nexus run --json` prints (`RunJsonOutput` in `cli/src/run_contract.ts`: a result, or an error document without `turn`). It is generated by the same `SchemaBuilder`, so everything above about open objects and type mapping applies to it.
330	
331	```bash
332	cd cli
333	node scripts/gen_run_result_schema.mjs           # rewrite docs/protocol/run-result-v1.schema.json
334	node scripts/gen_run_result_schema.mjs --check   # exit 1 when the schema or its ledger is stale
335	```
336	
337	Its ledger is `RUN_RESULT_VERSIONS.json`, entries `{ "schema_version", "schemaSha256", "note" }`, newest last, under the same rule: the newest entry carries the SHA-256 of the checked-in schema and the current `RUN_RESULT_SCHEMA_VERSION`. An added optional member keeps the version; a removed or renamed member, or a changed type or meaning, bumps it. The schema covers every type the result reaches (`TurnResult`, `ValidationReceipt` and the rest), so a change to any of them needs a regenerated schema and an entry. `cli/test/run_json_contract.test.ts` runs the comparison. The manual (`docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, "`nexus run --json`") describes the document and the exit codes.
338	
339	## The `nexus verify --json` document
340	
341	`verify-result-v1.schema.json` is the JSON Schema of the one document `nexus verify --json` prints and `--receipt <file>` writes (`VerifyJsonOutput` in `cli/src/verify_contract.ts`: a result with `verdict`, or an error document without one). It is generated by the same `SchemaBuilder`; the two command-result generators share `cli/scripts/result_schema.mjs`.
